Source patch for the GNU build layer only -- applies to os/unix/wrapper.c.

Why: Solaris's sbrk() returns memory that is already executable (no W^X
enforcement on the heap in this era). Linux enforces NX on brk-grown
memory, and QEMU linux-user's own translation additionally checks
PAGE_EXEC, so the dictionary -- entered as a function pointer immediately
after being copied in (wrapper.c:614, "Call the Forth interpreter as a
subroutine") -- SIGSEGVs on the first instruction fetch. Measured directly:
a minimal reproducer under qemu-sparc64 linux-user showed sbrk-only memory
faulting on execution, the same memory made executable via mprotect()
running correctly.

Fix: after the existing sbrk(), mprotect() the newly-grown region
PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE|PROT_EXEC. Strictly additive -- legal and a no-op on a
host where sbrk() memory is already RWX (Solaris), so one wrapper.c serves
both toolchains with no #ifdef. Page-rounds down from the pre-sbrk break
and up from the post-sbrk break; getmem()'s own alignment (32 KiB) is
coarser than a page, so the mprotect'd range is always a superset of the
returned dictionary region.

--- a/os/unix/wrapper.c	2009-03-24 18:26:02.000000000 +0100
+++ b/os/unix/wrapper.c
@@ -112,6 +112,7 @@
 
 #include <signal.h>
 
+#include <sys/mman.h>
 #include "wrapper.h"
 #include "xref.h"
 #include "defines.h"
@@ -762,13 +763,27 @@
 	long size;
 {
 	char *start;
+	char *prev_brk, *new_brk;
+	long pgsz, lo, hi;
 
+	prev_brk = (char *)sbrk(0);
 	start = (char *)sbrk(size+DICT_ORIGIN_ALIGNMENT+DICT_HEADER_SIZE);
 
 	if (start == (char *)-1) {
 		error("forth: couldn't get memory", "");
 		exit(1);
 	}
+
+	new_brk = (char *)sbrk(0);
+	pgsz = sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE);
+	lo = (long)prev_brk & ~(pgsz - 1);
+	hi = ((long)new_brk + pgsz - 1) & ~(pgsz - 1);
+	if (mprotect((void *)lo, (size_t)(hi - lo),
+	    PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE | PROT_EXEC) != 0) {
+		error("forth: mprotect(PROT_EXEC) on dictionary memory failed", "");
+		exit(1);
+	}
+
 	return ((char *)((ulong_t)
 	    ROUNDUP(start+DICT_HEADER_SIZE, DICT_ORIGIN_ALIGNMENT)
 	    - DICT_HEADER_SIZE));
